---
import LegalLayout from "@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import {
  CONTACT_EMAIL,
  DEVELOPER_NAME,
  GITHUB_ISSUES_URL,
  SITE_URL,
} from "@/lib/constants";

const updated = "October 14, 2026";
---

<LegalLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  updated={updated}
  description="How Callisto, a macOS menu bar app for Google Calendar meetings, accesses, uses, stores, shares, protects, and deletes your Google user data."
>
  <h2>Who We Are</h2>
  <p>
    This Privacy Policy applies to Callisto, a menu bar app for macOS that shows
    and manages your meetings from Google Calendar, and to the Callisto website
    at <a href={`${SITE_URL}/`}>callisto.frami.dev</a>. Callisto is developed
    and maintained by {DEVELOPER_NAME} ("we", "us"). You can contact us about this
    policy at <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Summary</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Callisto runs entirely on your Mac. We do not operate servers that
      receive, store, or process your Google user data, and we never see it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Callisto reads your Google Calendar data only to show your meetings in the
      menu bar, and changes events only when you ask it to.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your Google user data is not sold, not used for advertising, not shared
      with third parties, and not used to train AI or machine learning models.
    </li>
    <li>
      You can delete all of it at any time by removing the account in Callisto
      and revoking access in your Google Account.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Google User Data Callisto Accesses</h2>
  <p>
    When you connect a Google account, Callisto asks Google for the permissions
    (OAuth scopes) listed below. Google lets you grant or decline each one on
    the consent screen. Callisto accesses only the data covered by the
    permissions you grant.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Basic profile</strong> (<code
        >https://www.googleapis.com/auth/userinfo.email</code
      >, <code>https://www.googleapis.com/auth/userinfo.profile</code>): your
      Google account ID, email address, and display name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar list</strong> (<code
        >https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.calendarlist.readonly</code
      >): the calendars in your Google Calendar list, with their IDs, names,
      colors, time zones, and your access role on each calendar.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar events, read</strong> (<code
        >https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events.readonly</code
      >): the events on those calendars from the start of today through about
      the next 31 days. For each event this includes the title, description,
      start and end times, time zone, location, status, recurrence rules,
      organizer, attendees (names, email addresses, and their responses), your
      own response, reminders, attachment names and links, conference and
      meeting links (for example Google Meet or Zoom), and the event's Google
      Calendar link.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Calendar events, write</strong> (<code
        >https://www.googleapis.com/auth/calendar.events</code
      >): optional. Used only when you choose an event action in Callisto. If
      you decline it, Callisto still shows your events and hides the edit
      actions.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Callisto does not access your Gmail, Google Drive, contacts, or any other
    Google service. It does not read the contents of attachments.
  </p>

  <h2>How Callisto Uses Google User Data</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto uses Google user data only to provide the features you see in the
    app:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your profile data identifies and labels each connected account in the
      Settings window, and lets you connect more than one account.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your calendar list lets you choose which calendars to show, and gives each
      event its calendar's color.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your events are shown as a list of upcoming meetings in the menu bar, with
      the next meeting in the menu bar title. Meeting links let you join a call
      with one click. Attendee responses show who is coming.
    </li>
    <li>
      With the optional write permission, Callisto changes an event only when
      you choose an action:
      <ul>
        <li>RSVP: sets your response to Accept, Maybe, or Decline.</li>
        <li>
          Delete or remove: deletes the event, or a single occurrence of a
          repeating event. For "this and following events", Callisto ends the
          series before the chosen occurrence by updating its recurrence rule.
        </li>
      </ul>
      These actions use Google's normal notification setting, so Google may email
      the organizer and other attendees about the change, as Google Calendar itself
      does.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Callisto never creates events, and never changes events without an action
    from you. We do not use Google user data for advertising, for credit or
    lending decisions, to build user profiles or databases, or for any purpose
    that is not part of the features above. No person reads your data. Callisto
    has no servers, so we cannot access it.
  </p>

  <h2>AI and Machine Learning</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto does not use Google user data, or any data obtained through Google
    Workspace APIs, to develop, improve, or train generalized or
    non-personalized artificial intelligence or machine learning models.
    Callisto does not transfer Google user data to any third-party AI tool or
    model.
  </p>

  <h2>Limited Use</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto's use and transfer to any other app of information received from
    Google APIs will adhere to the
    <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      >Google API Services User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>How Google User Data Is Stored</h2>
  <p>All Google user data stays on your Mac:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      OAuth access and refresh tokens are stored only in the macOS Keychain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Account, calendar, and event data are stored in Callisto's local database
      (SwiftData) in your user account's app container on your Mac.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your choices about which accounts and calendars to show are stored in the
      same local database. Settings such as sync interval and days to show are
      stored in the app's local preferences (UserDefaults).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Callisto has no backend. Your Google user data is never uploaded to us or to
    any server other than Google's.
  </p>

  <h2>Sharing, Transfer, and Disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto sends requests directly from your Mac to Google's servers to sign
    you in, refresh your access, read calendars and events, and apply the
    changes you choose. That is the only place your Google user data goes.
  </p>
  <p>
    We do not sell, rent, share, transfer, or disclose Google user data to
    anyone, including advertisers, data brokers, information resellers, or
    analytics providers. We would disclose data only if the law required it, but
    because we never receive or hold your Google user data, we have none to
    disclose.
  </p>

  <h2>Data Protection</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      All traffic between Callisto and Google uses HTTPS (TLS). Sign-in uses
      Google's OAuth 2.0 flow with PKCE in your default browser, so Callisto
      never sees your Google password.
    </li>
    <li>
      OAuth tokens are kept in the macOS Keychain, which encrypts them, and are
      never written to the database, preferences, or logs.
    </li>
    <li>
      Local data is protected by macOS user-account isolation and, when enabled,
      FileVault disk encryption.
    </li>
    <li>
      Callisto asks for the narrowest scopes it needs, and makes write access
      optional.
    </li>
    <li>
      Diagnostic messages that Callisto writes to the local macOS system log
      never contain event details or tokens. They can contain Google account and
      calendar identifiers (a calendar identifier can be an email address). They
      stay on your Mac and are not sent to us.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Retention and Deletion</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto keeps Google user data on your Mac only while the account is
    connected. Each sync replaces the cached data with the current data from
    Google, so events deleted in Google Calendar are also deleted locally.
  </p>
  <p>You can delete your data at any time:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Remove the account in Callisto:</strong> open Settings, find the account,
      and choose Remove. Callisto immediately deletes that account's OAuth tokens
      from the Keychain and its profile, calendars, and events from the local database.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Revoke Callisto's access in Google:</strong> go to
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections"
        >Google Account → Third-party apps &amp; services</a
      >, select Callisto, and remove access. Removing the account in Callisto
      does not revoke this permission on its own, so do this too if you stop
      using Callisto.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Uninstall:</strong> quit Callisto, delete it from Applications, and
      delete its data folder,
      <code>~/Library/Containers/dev.frami.callisto</code>. Then remove any
      Callisto items from Keychain Access.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because we hold no copy of your Google user data, there is nothing for us to
    delete on our side. If you need help deleting your data, email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> and we will walk you through
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>App Updates</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto checks for updates with the Sparkle framework, which downloads an
    update feed and update files from GitHub. These requests contain the app
    version and normal connection data, such as your IP address. They contain no
    Google user data. GitHub handles them under the
    <a
      href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement"
      >GitHub Privacy Statement</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics and Tracking</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto has no advertising, analytics, crash reporting, or tracking code.
  </p>

  <h2>This Website</h2>
  <p>
    callisto.frami.dev is a static website hosted on Cloudflare. It sets no
    cookies of its own and runs no analytics. Cloudflare processes ordinary
    connection data, such as IP address, user agent, and requested URL, to serve
    and protect the site, under the
    <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/"
      >Cloudflare Privacy Policy</a
    >. Downloads are served from GitHub.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    Callisto is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly
    collect data from them.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
  <p>
    We will update this page when Callisto changes how it accesses, uses,
    stores, or shares Google user data, and change the "Last updated" date
    above. If a change is material, we will publish it here and announce it in
    the app's release notes before the new use takes effect, and ask for your
    consent where required.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    For privacy questions or data requests, email
    <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. You can also open
    an issue on <a href={GITHUB_ISSUES_URL}>GitHub</a>, but do not post personal
    data there.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
